courses.learning_outcomes:
Upon completion of the course, students are able to:---

Demonstrate understanding in the basic notions of commutative algebra, and the basic and important results in commutative algebra;

Demonstrate familiarity with tools from homological algebra and category theory which are directly applicable in the study of commutative algebra.

Upon completion of the course, students are able to:---

Demonstrate ability in defining and recognizing analytic functions;

Demonstrate understanding in the basic and important results analytic functions, and ability in providing arguments for these results;

Demonstrate understanding the important results in local and global theories for analytic functions;

Demonstrate ability in performing calculations with methods provided by the theory of analytic functions.

Upon completion of the course, students are able to:---

Demonstrate understanding in the notions of normed linear spaces and inner product spaces, of bounded linear operators, functionals and dual spaces, and also of various types of convergence in a normed linear space, and the basic properties of these objects and concepts;

Demonstrate understanding in the notion of orthogonality and direct sums in inner product spaces, and their relevance in Fourier series and other classical orthogonal series;

Demonstrate understanding in the Riesz Representation Theorem on Hilbert space and in rudiments of the theory of self-adjoint operators;

Demonstrate understanding in rudiments of various advanced topics in functional analysis such as spectrum, compact operators, Fredholm alternatives, positive operators, and their applications (such as in the study of differential equations and of measure theory).

Upon completion of the course, students are able to:---

Demonstrate understanding in the notion of smooth manifolds, and various basic concepts and constructions associated to the notion of smooth manifolds;

Demonstrate ability in performing computations concerned with various basic concepts and constructions associated to the notion of smooth manifolds in concrete situations.

Upon completion of the course, students are able to:---

Demonstrate understanding in basic properties of ordinary differential equations and partial differential equations (and initial/boundary value problems involving such equations);

Demonstrate ability in applying simple analytic and geometric methods for the study of ODE’s and PDE’s (and initial/boundary value problems involving such equations), with or without finding explicit solutions.

Upon completion of the course, students are able to:---

Demonstrate understanding in fundamental concepts involved in numerical analysis, including those for differential equations;
Demonstrate understanding in basic approaches in solving linear and nonlinear systems, constructing interpolations, doing numerical differentiation and integration, and solving ordinary and partial differential equations, and apply the techniques in handling these problems.

Upon completion of the course, students are able to:---

Demonstrate understanding in various topics in modern data analysis and the technical difficulties in solving them;

Demonstrate understanding in the mathematical and theoretical foundation of computational data analytics;

Demonstrate understanding in how to use various mathematical models to extract important information from big data;

Demonstrate ability in evaluating systematically the performance of different models to solve various data analysis problems;

Demonstrate understanding in the importance of computational data analytics for real-world problems;

Demonstrate understanding in some classical analysis techniques in statistical learning theory;

Demonstrate ability in apply the skills in learning theory.
